                "entry_id": "6dcf1e6c-880d-3299-9d5b-da3f18411322",
                "word": "homotopy",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "33428ab2-4bcb-42ae-ae45-b46165248c46",
                        "entry_id": "6dcf1e6c-880d-3299-9d5b-da3f18411322",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "homotopies"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "34cc8d2c-c84f-3501-89c5-f86bd7ae0d70",
                        "entry_id": "6dcf1e6c-880d-3299-9d5b-da3f18411322",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A continuous deformation of one continuous function or map to another."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "51dbe0fd-b02a-4071-94ef-45370a5c9120",
                                "sense_id": "34cc8d2c-c84f-3501-89c5-f86bd7ae0d70",
                                "text": "The concept of homotopy represents a formalisation of the intuitive idea of a smooth deformation of one curve into another."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f0e57917-2f12-4816-9880-c963f84c6173",
                                "sense_id": "34cc8d2c-c84f-3501-89c5-f86bd7ae0d70",
                                "text": "An integer M is called an exponent for the torsion of an abelian group G if M * (torsion of G) = 0. We say that M is a homotopy exponent for a space X if M is an exponent for πₖ (X) for all k."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8342f431-4d6b-47d6-b925-7bedc20b1301",
                                "sense_id": "34cc8d2c-c84f-3501-89c5-f86bd7ae0d70",
                                "text": "A graded Lie algebra arises from these maps via the Samelson product in homotopy, the so-called homotopy Lie algebra which is discussed below."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "02a4f44d-2445-481d-b5fe-4090f902b04e",
                                "sense_id": "34cc8d2c-c84f-3501-89c5-f86bd7ae0d70",
                                "text": "2010, Vladimir G. Turaev, Homotopy Quantum Field Theory, European Mathematical Society, page xi,\nIn this monograph we apply the idea of a TQFT to maps from manifolds to topological spaces. This leads us to a notion of a (d + 1)-dimensional homotopy quantum field theory (HQFT) which may be described as a TQFT for closed oriented d-dimensional manifolds and compact oriented (d + 1)-dimensional cobordisms endowed with maps to a given space X."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "37538863-5e71-3ffd-b684-031a4e86b71d",
                        "entry_id": "6dcf1e6c-880d-3299-9d5b-da3f18411322",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The relationship between two continuous functions where homotopy from one to the other is evident."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a33c33b3-08c2-36e9-8717-ff2b651ae71e",
                        "entry_id": "6dcf1e6c-880d-3299-9d5b-da3f18411322",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Ellipsis of homotopy theory (“the systematic study of homotopies and their equivalence classes”)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "abbreviation",
                            "alt-of",
                            "countable",
                            "ellipsis",
                            "informal",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
